// @emit-error a map value is not a supported union member
// THE SECOND HALF of the map-alias defect, pinned where it now lands. The sibling case
// `alias-declaration-parses.vl` removed the PARSE gate; this is what the gate was hiding.
//
// The parser encodes every non-`{`-bodied `type N = …` as a ONE-MEMBER `UnionDecl`, and a
// map body now takes that same path. `typecheck.singleAliasMemberTyIx` is what decides
// whether such a degenerate wrapper is TRANSPARENT — it is for a `TyPrim` member
// (`type Id = i32`), for a `TyFunc` member (D-ALIASFN), and for a `TyArray` member over a
// scalar spine (D-ALIASARR) — and its header states in as many words that `TyMap` is
// deliberately NOT included, "the same shape of hole … but it has its own twin table to
// build and its own value-name family to check". That deferral was unobservable while the
// construct could not be spelled at all; now it can, so here is its pin.
//
// So `Counts` denotes `TyUnion[TyMap]`, not `TyMap`, and the emitter's union machinery
// rejects it cleanly and by name. The failure is loud at EVERY use, never a miscompile:
// censused over 41 cells, a map alias in annotation position gives this emit error or a
// positioned checker error ("cannot index non-array {[string]: i32}"), and 0 cells emit an
// invalid module.
//
// FILED, with the probe that proves it: adding `if mt is TyMap { return m0 }` to the arm
// ladder in `typecheck.singleAliasMemberTyIx` moves 35 of those 41 cells to OK — measured on
// a probe build, not shipped, because `typecheck.vl` is another partition's file. The 2 cells
// that still fail under that probe are an array OF maps (`M[]`) with an f64 or string value,
// and that one is NOT the alias's doing: the alias-free inline control
// `const xs: {[string]: f64}[] = []` emits an invalid module on master too.
//
// When that arm lands, this case becomes `@run` — print `1`.
type Counts = {[string]: i32}
const _m: Counts = Map()
print(1)
